+# Bun migration
+
+Requires Bun 1.4.4 (the supplied executable was used for validation).
+
+```sh
+bun install --frozen-lockfile
+bun run typecheck
+bun run check
+bun run serve /absolute/path/to/static/root
+```
+
+`serve` retains its root directory argument and listens only on `127.0.0.1`, on
+an automatically assigned port. It prints one JSON object containing `port` on
+stdout after binding. `check` remains available as an alias for the real strict
+TypeScript `typecheck` script. The original project had no run, test, or build
+tasks or external dependencies. TypeScript 7.0.2 and Bun types 1.4.4 are pinned
+new development dependencies; bun.lock pins their transitive dependencies.
+
+The health, POST echo, static-file, and fallback routes retain their status
+codes and bodies. Real-path resolution and the root-plus-separator containment
+check retain the existing treatment of symlinks and traversal. Missing files,
+directories, and malformed percent escapes return 404. An existing file resolved
+outside the root returns 403. The original resolve-then-read filesystem race
+remains; serve a trusted, stable directory.
+
+Bun infers different default content types than Deno. JSON and error responses
+therefore specify the original content types explicitly. Static files use a
+single-chunk byte stream, preserving binary
+bytes and the original absence of a content-type header.
+
+## Intentional runtime and security differences
+
+Deno's `--allow-read --allow-net=127.0.0.1` permissions have no equivalent in
+this Bun launch command. Bun runs with the operating system user's filesystem,
+network, environment, and process privileges. The explicit loopback bind and
+static-root containment remain, but they are not a runtime permission sandbox.
+Use operating-system isolation if the former Deno permission restrictions are
+required. No Deno executable or configuration is used by the migrated project.
+
+HTTP transport behavior (such as connection management, default request-size
+limits, timeout handling, transfer framing, and generated Date headers) is supplied by Bun rather
+than Deno. This migration retains Bun's transport defaults. Bun's default maximum
+request body is 128 MiB; larger echo requests may be rejected before the handler.
+Static responses can use chunked transfer encoding rather than Content-Length.
+Bun development error pages are disabled to avoid exposing source details on
+unhandled errors.
+
+## Validation
+
+Strict `bun run typecheck` and the retained `bun run check` pass. An independent
+local harness compared the original Deno server with `bun run serve` for 19
+requests, checking status, raw body, and content type. Cases
+covered health, UTF-8 and empty POST echo, wrong methods, fallback, text and
+binary and empty files, static POST, health HEAD, percent-encoded names, missing
+paths, malformed escapes and NUL,
+directories, internal and external symlinks, and encoded traversal into a
+sibling directory. Startup JSON and root-argument forwarding were also checked.
+No pre-existing tests or skipped assertions existed to port.
